Functional Heartburn
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Retrosternal heartburn, Lack of response to PPIs, Normal pH-impedance monitoring, Normal endoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-018
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe retrosternal burning pain causing severe distress and fear of an acute myocardial infarction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with severe, unrelenting retrosternal chest discomfort radiating to the neck, unresponsive to maximal antacid therapy. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to perform a 12-lead ECG, assess hemodynamics, and definitively exclude acute coronary syndrome or a perforated viscus before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My chest is completely on fire behind my breastbone, I've taken four reflux pills and it's doing absolutely nothing. I'm scared it's my heart."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 3-hour episode of severe burning retrosternal discomfort. The pain does not strictly correlate with meals but flared tonight following an argument (severe psychosocial stress). The patient reports a total lack of adequate clinical response to maximal proton pump inhibitor therapy (Esomeprazole 40mg twice daily) taken today. Patient denies significant progressive dysphagia, unintentional weight loss, diaphoresis, or exertional dyspnea. Preserved normal appetite.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely anxious and sitting upright continuously.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; intense cardiac anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Anxious, rubbing the center of the chest, appearing uncomfortable but not acutely toxic.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals regular rate and rhythm, normal S1/S2, with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. Chest wall is non-tender to palpation. Abdomen is completely soft, non-tender, with no epigastric guarding or peritonism. Documented history shows a completely normal upper endoscopy and normal 24-hour ambulatory pH-impedance monitoring, confirming a total absence of acid or non-acid reflux events driving the symptoms.
Environmental Observations: Empty blister packs of high-dose PPIs and Gaviscon on the coffee table.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking maximum dose Esomeprazole with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Functional Heartburn - Status: Acute nociceptive flare
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out by normal 12-lead ECG performed on-site, absence of exertional triggers, lack of diaphoresis, and localized burning rather than crushing pressure.

Gastro-oesophageal Reflux Disease (GORD) - Ruled out explicitly by the documented normal pH-impedance monitoring and absolute failure of high-dose PPIs.

Oesophageal Spasm - Unlikely given the burning nature of the pain (as opposed to severe cramping/squeezing) and previously normal motility studies.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to severe, acute retrosternal chest pain which clinically carries a high risk of life-threatening cardiac ischemia. An immediate, on-site evaluation, strictly including a 12-lead ECG and cardiovascular physical examination, was completely mandatory to rule out acute coronary syndrome. Diagnosing a functional gastrointestinal flare, based on the normal ECG and the explicitly documented history of normal pH monitoring, safely prevented a highly distressed and potentially harmful emergency department admission. Reassurance and appropriate cessation of ineffective high-dose antacids immediately stabilized the patient's anxiety, fulfilling the strict requirements for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed normal 12-lead ECG on-site. Advised to immediately cease overusing the ineffective high-dose PPIs to prevent medication-induced rebound or side effects.
Positive Behavioral Change: When the retrosternal burning starts, execute 10 minutes of diaphragmatic breathing instead of instantly swallowing antacids. Deep, rhythmic breathing mechanically relaxes the diaphragm and decreases central nervous system hyperarousal, rewarding the patient with an organic reduction in symptom severity and building reliance on self-regulation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to discuss transitioning from PPIs to a low-dose SNRI or TCA for oesophageal hypersensitivity.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the pain changes character to a crushing, heavy weight, radiates down the left arm or jaw, or is accompanied by sudden sweating, shortness of breath, or fainting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: An ECG is a snapshot in time; any sudden change in the nature or severity of the chest pain requires an immediate re-evaluation.
Clinical Course: Functional heartburn is a neuropathic pain of the oesophagus. Because there is no actual acid burning the tissue, traditional acid-blocking medications will perpetually fail. Treatment requires neuromodulation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if the burning turns into a heavy, crushing pressure, if you break out in a cold sweat, or if you feel severe breathlessness.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your previous tests proved there is exactly zero acid refluxing into your oesophagus. The nerves themselves are misfiring and sending a false 'burn' signal to the brain. We must stop treating non-existent acid and start calming the nerves.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking the extra Gaviscon and PPIs; they are providing no benefit. Implement the deep breathing technique every time pain spikes. Seeing the pain decrease through relaxation rather than medication will reinforce the true functional nature of the condition.

